All 21 candidates verified: 19 confirmed, 1 plausible, 1 refuted (the abuse-warning surface — it's deliberately in the 201 body and the GUI shows it). After merging the two `withDefaults` zero-vs-absent findings and cutting to the 10 most severe (correctness first):

```json
[
  {
    "file": "cmd/forgemark/serve.go",
    "line": 38,
    "summary": "isLoopback treats an empty host as loopback, so `forgemark serve -addr :8377` wildcard-binds all interfaces with the README-promised security warning suppressed.",
    "failure_scenario": "Operator runs `forgemark serve -addr :8377`; net.SplitHostPort yields host \"\", isLoopback returns true and no warning prints, yet http.Server listens on 0.0.0.0 — anyone on the LAN can reach the control panel, which both accepts pasted tokens and can mint fresh ones via secret_source exec of `gh auth token`/`entire auth token`."
  },
  {
    "file": "scripts/compare-local.sh",
    "line": 196,
    "summary": "The run-start failure branch re-executes the identical POST /api/runs just to print the error body, which can silently start a real duplicate benchmark run.",
    "failure_scenario": "First `curl -sf` POST fails transiently (e.g. read timeout after the server accepted); the fallback `curl -s` in the error echo POSTs the same $BODY again — a sustained load run against real forges (github.com by default) starts while the script discards its ID, prints an 'error', and exits 1 with no way to watch or cancel it (or the retry 409s, masking the original error)."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/server/static/js/views/newrun.js",
    "line": 176,
    "summary": "Fully non-numeric concurrency input parses to [] and the server's withDefaults silently substitutes the default sweep [1,8,32,128] instead of rejecting the request.",
    "failure_scenario": "User types 'abc' (or any non-numeric text) in the concurrency field; split/parseInt/filter yields [], withDefaults (runmgr.go:58) fills [1,8,32,128] before Validate runs, and the run sweeps up to 128 concurrent agents — against github.com that is exactly the abuse-limit scenario the UI warns about, with no error shown."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/server/runmgr.go",
    "line": 82,
    "summary": "withDefaults unconditionally coerces CloneDepth 0 to 1 (making the preceding strategy-gated default dead code), so the documented 'clone_depth 0 = full history' is unrepresentable via the server API.",
    "failure_scenario": "User selects clone/session strategy in the GUI and enters 0 in the 'Clone depth (0 = full)' field; the server rewrites it to 1 and agents run tip-only shallow clones while the UI and the persisted results doc claim a full-history clone workload — silently measuring the wrong thing."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/server/demo.go",
    "line": 113,
    "summary": "demoRunner synthesizes every sample with the zero-value Op (OpPush) regardless of workload strategy, so demo:// targets produce no clone samples under clone/session strategies.",
    "failure_scenario": "New run with strategy=clone plus 'Add demo pair': the collector counts all samples as pushes but every consumer reads clone_* fields for clone strategy, so live tiles, charts, and race lanes show 0/'–' for the whole run, and the final LevelResult is all zeros because summarize's clone remap swaps in Clone* counters that were never incremented."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/server/runmgr.go",
    "line": 493,
    "summary": "emitBucket copies only push-side counters into results.SeriesPoint and drops all clone_* fields, so a clone-strategy run's persisted per-second series is entirely zeros.",
    "failure_scenario": "Run a clone-strategy GUI benchmark: all samples are OpClone so the persisted series has ok=0/p50=0 every second; the History timeline replay renders a flat zero throughput line and an empty latency chart while the sweep charts next to them (from LevelResult, which summarize remaps for clone) show hundreds of clones/s."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/server/static/js/views/race.js",
    "line": 252,
    "summary": "race.js branches only on strategy==='clone', so for session strategy (which both clones and pushes) clone failures and clone latency are invisible in every race metric, including reliability.",
    "failure_scenario": "A session-strategy race where one forge times out most clones but pushes fine: race.js reads only v.ok/v.err/v.cas, so lane.attempts excludes clones entirely and the reliability lane shows near-100% for a forge that is failing half its workload — the dashboard's dashed clone series shows the failures, the race verdict contradicts it."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/server/runmgr.go",
    "line": 64,
    "summary": "withDefaults conflates JSON-absent with explicit zero: an omitted warmup_sec yields 0s warmup (CLI default is 10s, and only a -1 sentinel no client sends gets the default), while explicit zeros for files_min/file_size/duration_sec are silently rewritten to defaults where the CLI hard-errors.",
    "failure_scenario": "A minimal curl POST omitting warmup_sec runs with zero warm-up, polluting measured percentiles with cold-start samples so server results silently aren't comparable to the CLI's for the 'same' workload — contradicting the struct doc 'Zero fields take the CLI's flag defaults'; separately, files_min:0 (a CLI validation error) silently runs a 1-10-file workload the user never requested."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/server/events.go",
    "line": 54,
    "summary": "emit silently drops events at the maxBufferedEvents cap — including the terminal run_done — instead of evicting old ones, so clients never learn a long run finished.",
    "failure_scenario": "A soak run of ~28h fills the 100k-event log; run_done is discarded, and since clients only settle on the run_done event (log.close() ends the stream but onerror just shows 'reconnecting…'), the browser reconnect-loops forever, the badge stays 'running', and the cancel button stays live on a finished run."
  },
  {
    "file": "internal/server/static/js/charts.js",
    "line": 91,
    "summary": "timeChart.push() re-feeds the entire accumulated data arrays into uPlot setData on every bucket event, and SSE replay dispatches every historical bucket individually — three full-history redraws per replayed event, O(n²) total.",
    "failure_scenario": "Opening or reconnecting to a dashboard 30+ minutes into a run replays ~1800 bucket events × 3 charts × full-array setData (~5400 full redraws plus per-event tile writes), freezing the tab for seconds and growing quadratically with run length; coalescing replay into one setData on catch-up (e.g. rAF-batched) removes it."
  }
]
```

Nine confirmed findings didn't make the top-10 cut, worth a line each: the CLI's `writeResults` hand-mirrors `results.Doc`'s schema (drift silently breaks History parsing of CLI docs); `secret_source` resolution execs CLIs serially inside the POST handler (up to ~30s stall); `suggestEntire` runs its two CLI calls sequentially; `suggestEntire` doesn't validate the parsed handle (can suggest `et/forgemark-/forgemark-target`); `bench.Target.Secret` says "Never serialized" but lacks `json:"-"` (latent, no current leak path); `compare-local.sh` re-implements the discovery the server already exposes at `/api/local/suggest`; race/dashboard duplicate countdown math and the results-saved banner; `sample`/`Sample` are identical structs with copy loops; `BucketStats.Ops/Clones` are emitted but never read.

